        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user is asking whether the export table of a DLL is the .lib file. Let me clarify.\n\nNo, the export table is NOT in the .lib. The export table is **inside the .dll itself**.\n\nThe .lib (import library) is a separate small file that contains just enough information for the linker to know:\n- Which DLL to load\n- Which symbols are available from that DLL\n- How to set up the IAT in the .exe\n\nThe DLL itself contains a section called `.edata` (export data) which IS the actual export table — it lists all exported symbols and their addresses within the DLL.\n\nSo:\n- `.dll` contains the **export table** (real table with addresses)\n- `.lib` (import library) contains **reference information** (name of DLL + names of exported symbols) for the linker to create the IAT in .exe\n\nThe analogy:\n- Export table (in .dll) = menu in a restaurant kitchen\n- Import library (.lib) = a note to your friend saying \"go to restaurant X and order pasta\"",
