          "text": "The user is summarizing their understanding of the DLL/import library/IAT mechanism and wants confirmation. Let me verify their understanding.\n\nThey said:\n1. Inside DLL there's its own export table (of functions visible from outside)\n2. Inside the executable there's IAT (Import Address Table)\n3. There's a .lib (import library) that helps the linker - it contains a mapping table that says \"function X is at address Y in DLL Z\"\n4. The linker writes this data into the IAT\n\nLet me check point 3 more carefully. The import .lib doesn't contain *addresses* of functions. It contains:\n- The name of the DLL\n- The names/ordinals of the exported functions\n\nThe linker reads this and creates the IAT in the .exe. At load time, the Windows loader resolves the actual addresses from the DLL's export table and writes them into the IAT.\n\nSo the user's description is slightly off: the import .lib doesn't contain addresses (those are only known when the DLL is loaded at runtime). The import .lib contains the DLL name and the function names (or ordinals). The linker records in the .exe's IAT: \"when loaded, find function `parse_point` from `common.dll`\". The actual address is resolved at load time by the Windows loader.\n\nBut the overall sequence is correctly understood:\n1. Linker reads import .lib → creates IAT in .exe (with DLL name + function names)\n2. At runtime, Windows loader: reads .exe's IAT → loads the DLL → reads DLL's export table → matches function names → writes addresses into .exe's IAT\n3. Then main() runs and can call the function",
