          "text": "The user is confused about terminology. Let me clarify:\n\nThe scenario with circular dependency A↔B:\n\n1. When building **A.dll**, the linker needs to know that `get_geometry` is in **B.dll**. That's an **import** in A.dll's perspective — A needs to import `get_geometry` from B.\n   \n2. When building **B.dll**, the linker needs to know that `log_message` is in **A.dll**. That's an **import** in B.dll's perspective.\n\nBut wait, actually the `.exp` file is about the **export** side of the current DLL, not the import side. Let me think about this more carefully.\n\nWhen you run `link /DLL /OUT:A.dll A.obj B.exp`:\n- `A.obj` contains calls to `get_geometry` which is in `B.dll` — that's an import\n- `B.exp` is the export file from `B.dll` that says \"B.dll exports `get_geometry`\"\n\nThe `.exp` file tells the linker about **exports from another DLL**, not the current one.\n\nActually wait, let me re-read my earlier explanation. I said:\n\n\"при сборке A.dll линкер создаёт .exp с черновиком таблицы экспорта A.dll. Потом при сборке B.dll линкер видит A.exp, понимает «ага, log_message будет в A.dll»\"\n\nSo the situation is:\n1. First you build A.dll — this creates A.exp (saying A.dll will export log_message and other functions)\n2. Then you build B.dll — you pass A.exp to the linker, so it knows log_message will eventually be in A.dll\n3. But B.dll calls get_geometry? No... let me re-think.\n\nActually, in the circular case:\n- A calls get_geometry from B → B must be built first so A can import from it\n- B calls log_message from A → A must be built first so B can import from it\n\nThis is the chicken-and-egg problem. The solution with `.exp`:\n\n1. First pass: build A.dll partially — create A.exp with its exports, but don't resolve imports yet\n2. Build B.dll with A.exp — B knows log_message is in A.dll, can resolve that import\n3. Now build A.dll fully — B is ready, so A can resolve its import of get_geometry\n\nActually, I think the typical flow is different. Let me check...\n\nIn MSVC:\n1. Build A.dll with `/DEF` or exports → creates A.exp, A.lib (import lib), A.dll\n2. Build B.dll — pass A.exp or A.lib as input\n3. Build A's consumer — use A.lib (import lib)\n\nWait, actually `.exp` is generated by the linker when creating a DLL that exports functions. It's used when another DLL wants to import from this DLL during the same build session.\n\nLet me simplify. The `.exp` file is just a backup/helper. In most simple cases (no circular dependencies), `.exp` is indeed useless after the DLL build is done.\n\nThe user's question is: why did I say the `.exp` is about **exports** when the problem is about **importing** (A needs something from B)?\n\nThe answer is: `.exp` describes what the **current** DLL exports. When you build A.dll, the linker creates A.exp listing what A.dll exports (like log_message). When you later build B.dll, you pass A.exp to the linker so it knows that log_message comes from A.dll. From B's perspective, log_message is an **import** — but the reference that resolves it is A.exp, which describes A's **exports**.\n\nSo `.exp` = \"here's what **I** export\" (from the current DLL's perspective), which helps **other** DLLs resolve their imports.\n\nLet me explain this to the user clearly.",
