de.\n\nWait, actually `__declspec(dllimport)` in MSVC does affect code generation - it tells the compiler to generate an indirect call via the IAT. If the function is actually statically linked, this could cause incorrect behavior or linker errors.\n\nThe proper approach for a header shared between static and dynamic builds:\n1. Use a second macro name, like `STATIC_BUILD`, to disable `COMMON_API` when building statically\n2. Or define `COMMON_API` as empty for static builds\n\nBut the simpler approach: if t1/t2 are still static and include the same headers, they need the headers to know they're building statically. \n\nThe cleanest way: define another macro, like `COMMON_STATIC`, and conditionally make `COMMON_API` empty.\n\n```cpp\n#ifdef COMMON_STATIC\n#define COMMON_API\n#elif defined(COMMON_EXPORTS)\n#define COMMON_API __declspec(dllexport)\n#else\n#define COMMON_API __declspec(dllimport)\n#endif\n```\n\nThen for t1/t2 static builds, either:\n- Pass `/DCOMMON_STATIC` during compilation\n- Or don't, and accept the `dllimport` (which MSVC might handle for static linking with a warning)\n\nActually, the user's concern is valid. Let me think about what exactly happens.\n\nFor static linking with `__declspec(dllimport)`:\n- MSVC may generate a warning C4273: 'function' : inconsistent dll linkage\n- Or it might work silently depending on the version\n\nThe correct approach is to use the 3-state macro as I described.\n\nActually wait - does the user even still use static linking for t1/t2? Or did they convert them to DLL too? Let me think about what the current state likely is...\n\nThe user has been working through the layers sequentially on the C++ side. They converted t3 and t4 to DLL. For t1 and t2, they might still be using static or they might have been converted too.\n\nBut the actual question is simpler: \"tasks 1-2 include these headers, will they also get `__declspec` marking even though they don't need it?\" The answer is yes - any `.cpp` that includes `geometry.hpp` will see `COMMON_API` which expands to `__declspec(dllimport)`. Whether that's a problem depends on how t1/t2 are linked.\n\nIf t1/t2 link statically (with `common_cpp.lib` as a static lib), having `dllimport` in the header is incorrect and could cause issues.\n\nThe solution: add a third state to the macro for static builds.\n\nBut I'm in plan mode - I should explain the issue and propose a solution, not edit.",
